    "path": "content/ef2/module-02/lesson-02.md",
    "content": "# Lesson 02 — Participating in a Meeting\n\n> Source: `F2_M02_L02.pdf` — 5 pages\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M02_L02.pdf, page 1 -->\n\n## Warm-Up\n\n1. Why is it important to get someone to participate and share ideas in a meeting?\n\n2. Is it easy to get your colleagues to participate in meetings?\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M02_L02.pdf, page 2 -->\n\n## Role-Play\n\n### Scenario: David is asking for everyone’s ideas during the meeting.\n\n**David:** Alright, everyone. Does anyone have any ideas regarding our new project concept?\n\n**Pia:** We need to create something that will link our future products together and can be controlled via smartphone.\n\n**David:** That’s an interesting point, Pia. What do you think, Daniel?\n\n**Daniel:** Well, we don’t currently have anything like that, and I think there’s a lot of potential there. Pia, could you further clarify the information needed on that?\n\n**Pia:** We can use the information gathered for data analysis and statistics.\n\n**David:** Karla, do you have anything to add?\n\n**Karla:** That’s a great point, Pia. I think it could be extremely useful for us and for our customers. I’d like to add that we must create an entirely new system to manage the data.\n\n**David:** Thank you for your input, Karla. Does anyone else have any additional ideas?\n\n### Role-Play Analysis\n\nAnswer the following questions:\n\n1. What did you learn from this role-play?\n\n2. How did you feel about participating in the discussion?\n\n3. What would you do differently in a real meeting?\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M02_L02.pdf, page 3 -->\n\n## Participating in a Meeting Useful Phrases\n\n1. I’d like to add something – used to contribute additional information or insights to the discussion.\n\n### Example\n\n- I’d like to add something to that point.\n\n2. Could you clarify that? – used to seek additional information or explanation on a topic.\n\n### Example\n\n- Just to make sure I’m clear, could you clarify that point further?\n\n3. That’s an interesting point / That’s a great point – used to acknowledge and show appreciation for someone’s idea or comment.\n\n### Example\n\n- That’s an interesting point, thank you for sharing!\n\n- That’s a great point; can we explore it further?\n\n4. Can I have a word here? / May I jump in? – used to politely interrupt and contribute to the discussion.\n\n### Example\n\n- Excuse me, can I have a word here?\n\n- Excuse me, may I jump in?\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M02_L02.pdf, page 4 -->\n\n## Practice\n\n### Instructions\n\nTeam up with a partner and practice asking for input by making statement and then asking your partner for their input.\n\n| STATEMENT | ASK FOR INPUT |\n| --- | --- |\n| Our brand deserves to be recognized by<br>more people. | Does anyone have any ideas about the<br>issue? |\n| Business attire during client meeting is<br>best. | What do you think, [Partner’s Name] |\n| We should have a strict work-life balance<br>policy. |   |\n| We had better allocate our resources on<br>the new system and technology. |   |\n\n## Production\n\nTo help you feel more confident and prepared when participating in meetings, answer the following questions.\n\n1. What should you do to prepare for a meeting?\n\n2. How can you confidently participate in a meeting?\n\n3. How can you effectively communicate your ideas in a meeting?\n\n4. How can you stay engaged and focused during a meeting?\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M02_L02.pdf, page 5 -->\n\n## Key Vocabulary\n\n| Word/Phrase | Use | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Contribute | To add or provide an idea,<br>suggestion, or expertise to<br>the discussion | Can I contribute my thoughts<br>on this topic? |\n| Ask for clarification | To ensure understanding of<br>the discussion | Can you clarify that point? |\n| Build on | To add or expand on<br>someone else’s idea or<br>suggestion | I would like to build on<br>Nelson’s idea. |\n| Interrupt politely | To add value to the<br>discussion without<br>interrupting aggressively | Excuse me, may I add<br>something? |\n| Summarize | To recap the main points<br>discussed | Let me summarize what we<br>have covered so far… |",
    "searchText": "ef2-m02-l02 ef2 module 02 lesson 02 participating in a meeting f2 m02 l02.pdf lesson 02 — participating in a meeting source: f2 m02 l02.pdf — 5 pages warm-up 1. why is it important to get someone to participate and share ideas in a meeting? 2. is it easy to get your colleagues to participate in meetings? role-play scenario: david is asking for everyone’s ideas during the meeting. david: alright, everyone. does anyone have any ideas regarding our new project concept? pia: we need to create something that will link our future products together and can be controlled via smartphone. david: that’s an interesting point, pia. what do you think, daniel? daniel: well, we don’t currently have anything like that, and i think there’s a lot of potential there. pia, could you further clarify the information needed on that? pia: we can use the information gathered for data analysis and statistics. david: karla, do you have anything to add? karla: that’s a great point, pia. i think it could be extremely useful for us and for our customers. i’d like to add that we must create an entirely new system to manage the data. david: thank you for your input, karla. does anyone else have any additional ideas? role-play analysis answer the following questions: 1. what did you learn from this role-play? 2. how did you feel about participating in the discussion? 3. what would you do differently in a real meeting? participating in a meeting useful phrases 1. i’d like to add something – used to contribute additional information or insights to the discussion. example - i’d like to add something to that point. 2. could you clarify that? – used to seek additional information or explanation on a topic. example - just to make sure i’m clear, could you clarify that point further? 3. that’s an interesting point / that’s a great point – used to acknowledge and show appreciation for someone’s idea or comment. example - that’s an interesting point, thank you for sharing! - that’s a great point; can we explore it further? 4. can i have a word here? / may i jump in? – used to politely interrupt and contribute to the discussion. example - excuse me, can i have a word here? - excuse me, may i jump in? practice instructions team up with a partner and practice asking for input by making statement and then asking your partner for their input. statement ask for input --- --- our brand deserves to be recognized by more people. does anyone have any ideas about the issue? business attire during client meeting is best. what do you think, [partner’s name] we should have a strict work-life balance policy. we had better allocate our resources on the new system and technology. production to help you feel more confident and prepared when participating in meetings, answer the following questions. 1. what should you do to prepare for a meeting? 2. how can you confidently participate in a meeting? 3. how can you effectively communicate your ideas in a meeting? 4. how can you stay engaged and focused during a meeting? key vocabulary word/phrase use example --- --- --- contribute to add or provide an idea, suggestion, or expertise to the discussion can i contribute my thoughts on this topic? ask for clarification to ensure understanding of the discussion can you clarify that point? build on to add or expand on someone else’s idea or suggestion i would like to build on nelson’s idea. interrupt politely to add value to the discussion without interrupting aggressively excuse me, may i add something? summarize to recap the main points discussed let me summarize what we have covered so far…"
