    "content": "# Lesson 02 — Writing Weekly and Monthly Reports\n\n> Source: `F2_M03_L02.pdf` — 6 pages\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M03_L02.pdf, page 1 -->\n\n## Warm-Up\n\n### Questions\n\n1. What types of reports have you had to write in the past (if any)?\n\n2. How often do you think reports should be written in a workplace environment?\n\n3. What information do you think is important to include in a report?\n\n4. What challenges do you face when writing reports?\n\n5. Why is it important to write clear and concise reports in a business setting?\n\n6. How do you ensure your reports are understood by all?\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M03_L02.pdf, page 2 -->\n\n## Sample Analysis\n\n**Directions:** Study the target phrases in the column and distinguish how they are different.\n\n| UNCLEAR REPORT |   |   | CLEAR REPORT |   |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| The findings of the monthly meeting says<br>many people are availing sick leave in<br>the office because of the virus in the<br>workplace. |   | The monthly report released this end<br>month of March found that 5% of<br>employees used their Sick Leave benefit<br>because of the proliferation of Flu Virus in<br>the Productions Department. |   |   |\n| Operations Manager Ted who is new to<br>the job this year mentioned we are all<br>lucky in the company that we have this<br>job still because of recession. |   | Another fact mentioned during the<br>Monthly meeting came from its Operations<br>Manager – Mr. Todd Philips who was<br>happy that the current job provides<br>opportunity despite the recession. |   |   |\n| One department is said to be terminated<br>because of poor production performance<br>that was said to be occurring for longer<br>times now. |   | Employees are encouraged to give their<br>best in the workplace because as<br>recession happens, thus the company<br>would want its best not to terminate<br>anybody. |   |   |\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M03_L02.pdf, page 3 -->\n\n## Reports Structure and Common Phrases\n\n|   | CONTENT |   |   |   | DESCRIPTION |   |   | PHRASES |   |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Title |   |   |   | Clearly state that it’s<br>a weekly or monthly<br>report. |   |   | “Weekly/Monthly Report: [Project<br>Name/Team Name]” |   |   |\n| Date |   |   |   | Include the date<br>range covered. |   |   | “Date Range: [Start Date] to [End<br>Date]”<br>“Reporting Period: [Start Date] -<br>[End Date]” |   |   |\n| Introduction |   |   |   | Briefly state the<br>purpose of the<br>report. |   |   | “This report aims to...”<br>“The purpose of this report is<br>to...” |   |   |\n| Main Body |   | Achievements |   | Highlight key<br>accomplishments. |   |   | \"During this period, we have<br>successfully achieved...\" |   |   |\n|   |   | Current<br>progress |   | Describe ongoing<br>tasks or projects. |   |   | “Currently, we are working on…” |   |   |\n|   |   | Challenges |   | Discuss any<br>challenges faced. |   |   | \"There were several obstacles<br>faced, including...\" |   |   |\n|   |   | Solutions |   | Explain the actions<br>taken to overcome<br>a challenge or<br>problem. |   |   | “To address this, we…” |   |   |\n|   |   | Next Steps |   | Outline what’s<br>planned for the<br>upcoming<br>week/month. |   |   | “Next, we plan to…”<br>“Our priority for the coming week<br>is…” |   |   |\n| Conclusion |   |   |   | Summarize the<br>overall progress. |   |   | “To conclude, the project is on<br>track with…” |   |   |\n| Signature |   |   |   | Include your name<br>and position if<br>applicable. |   |   | “Best regards,”<br>“Thank you,”<br>“Sincerely,” |   |   |\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M03_L02.pdf, page 4 -->\n\n## Practice\n\n**Directions:** Analyze the weekly report below and answer the following questions.\n\n**Subject:** Weekly Report (April 7-12, 2025) Dear Team,\n\n### This week, we achieved the following milestones\n\n- Completed the initial phase of the marketing campaign.\n\n- Held a successful client meeting with XYZ Corporation.\n\n- Resolved 95% of customer support tickets within 24 hours.\n\n### Next week, we plan to\n\n- Launch the second phase of the marketing campaign.\n\n- Conduct a training session for the sales team.\n\n- Improve response time for customer support tickets.\n\nBest regards,\n\nKevin Arnolds Planning Team 1 Manager\n\n### Questions\n\n1. What elements make this report clear and effective?\n\n2. What could be added to ensure all necessary information is included?\n\n3. What are some common mistakes to avoid when writing business reports?\n\n4. How can you make sure your report is straightforward yet informative?\n\n5. What are some strategies to ensure your report is visually appealing and easy to read?\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M03_L02.pdf, page 5 -->\n\n## Production\n\n### REPORT WRITING EXERCISE\n\n**Directions:** Write a weekly or monthly report based on the given scenarios below. Exchange reports and provide feedback on clarity and effectiveness.\n\n1. Choose a type of report\n\n- Project progress report\n\n- Sales performance report\n\n- Marketing campaign update\n\n- Financial summary\n\n2. Report Writing\n\n- Each participant writes a report based on their chosen scenario.\n\n- Don’t forget to incorporate these parts in your report.  Title: Clearly state the report's purpose.  Introduction: Brief overview of the report's content.  Body: Detailed information, including data, analysis, and key points.  Conclusion: Summary of findings and recommendations.  Appendices (if applicable): Additional data or supporting documents.\n\n### 3. Report Presentation and Feedback Review\n\n- Present your written report in the class.\n\n- Afterwards, exchange your reports for peer review. Use these guidelines for your feedback review. Use constructive and specific feedback.\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M03_L02.pdf, page 6 -->\n\n### Feedback Guidelines\n\n Clarity: Is the report easy to understand? Are the key points clearly stated?\n\n- Effectiveness: Does the report achieve its purpose? Are the recommendations actionable?\n\n- Structure: Is the report well-organized? Are the sections logically arranged?\n\n- Professionalism: Is the tone appropriate? Are there any grammatical or formatting errors?",
