    "content": "# Lesson 20 — Final Review\n\n> Source: `F2_M03_L20.pdf` — 5 pages\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M03_L20.pdf, page 1 -->\n\n## Warm-Up\n\n1. What words or phrases do you use to sound polite in emails?\n\n2. Do you give updates about your work?\n\n3. Have you ever had to apologize to a colleague or manager?\n\n4. What’s the purpose of a meeting agenda?\n\n5. Have you ever worked with a cross-functional team?\n\n6. Who usually leads meetings in your company?\n\n7. What do people negotiate at work?\n\n8. What happens when someone doesn’t understand their task?\n\n9. Do you prefer solving problems alone or with others?\n\n10. What is a “crisis” in the workplace?\n\n## Case-study\n\n### PROJECT SETBACK IN SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT\n\n**Background:** Tech Solutions Inc. is a software development company working on a new application intended to streamline project management for small businesses. The project started positively, but two months in, the team begins to face significant challenges that threaten the launch timeline.\n\n**Current Situation:** The development team has encountered multiple technical issues, including integration problems with third-party software and unexpected delays in feature implementation. Additionally, team morale has decreased as members are frustrated with the ongoing issues and experience communication breakdowns during\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M03_L20.pdf, page 2 -->\n\nteam meetings. The project manager, Laura, must address these challenges and bring the project back on track.\n\nCase-study Analysis\n\n1. What are the main challenges impacting the project?\n\n2. How can Laura improve communication among team members?\n\n3. What steps can be taken to address the integration issues?\n\n4. What strategies can be implemented to enhance team motivation and productivity?\n\n5. What measures can be adopted to prevent similar setbacks in future projects?\n\n## Practice\n\nA. Sort the following words into correct categories. (Each key word has one\n\nsynonym and one antonym.)\n\nIsolation Partnership Conflict Evaluation Settlement\n\nAgreement Compromise\n\n### Dialogue\n\nDispute Approach\n\nMisunderstanding Randomness Communication Consensus Neglect\n\n| Key Word | Synonym | Antonym |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Collaboration |   |   |\n| Negotiation |   |   |\n| Feedback |   |   |\n| Strategy |   |   |\n| Resolution |   |   |\n| Consensus |   |   |\n| Communication |   |   |\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M03_L20.pdf, page 3 -->\n\nB. Choose the best response for each situation.\n\n1. Which email subject line is most appropriate for a formal email?\n\nA. “Yo – Need That Report”\n\nB. “Quick thing”\n\nC. “Project Update – April 2025”\n\nD. “About That Stuff”\n\n2. Which sentence is best for a business report?\n\nA. “The team is kind of done.”\n\nB. “Things are going fine.”\n\nC. “Task A was completed on time. Task B is in progress.”\n\nD. “We are still working on something.”\n\n3. What is the politest and most professional request?\n\nA. “Send it now!”\n\nB. “Can you do this ASAP?”\n\nC. “Could you please send the report by Friday?”\n\nD. “You need to send it fast.”\n\n4. Which item is typically found in a meeting agenda?\n\nA. Weekend plans\n\nB. New project update\n\nC. Coffee orders\n\nD. Social events\n\n5. Which action helps improve communication in a team?\n\nA. Talk only with your manager\n\nB. Use clear written instructions\n\nC. Avoid meetings\n\nD. Work alone on everything\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M03_L20.pdf, page 4 -->\n\n6. How can you explain technical information to a non-technical person?\n\nA. Use only IT vocabulary\n\nB. Speak quickly\n\nC. Use simple, clear language and examples\n\nD. Show code only\n\n7. What’s a good strategy in cross-cultural meetings?\n\nA. Use slang\n\nB. Speak very fast\n\nC. Use clear and neutral language\n\nD. Assume everyone knows your culture\n\n8. What is a key role of the chairperson?\n\nA. Take personal notes\n\nB. Prepare snacks\n\nC. Manage the meeting and keep time\n\nD. Stay silent during discussion\n\n9. What helps make a virtual presentation more engaging?\n\nA. Speak without structure\n\nB. Read text without emotion\n\nC. Use visuals and ask questions\n\nD. Keep the screen blank\n\n10. What is a good goal in business negotiation?\n\nA. Win at any cost\n\nB. Make the other person lose\n\nC. Reach a solution that works for both sides\n\nD. Say “no” to everything\n\n<!-- Source: F2_M03_L20.pdf, page 5 -->\n\n## Production\n\n### Instructions\n\n- Use the target phrases below to create your own short dialogues or sentences.\n\n- You can work in pairs (for speaking) or individually (for writing).\n\n- Choose at least 5 phrases and use them in a realistic business situation (e.g. writing an email, solving a problem, or talking in a meeting).\n\n1. Could you please send me the report by...\n\n2. I’m writing to apologize for...\n\n3. Let’s schedule a meeting to discuss...\n\n4. The purpose of this email is to...\n\n5. Please let me know if you need any help.\n\n6. Here is a quick update on the project...\n\n7. We need to find a solution to...\n\n8. Thanks for your understanding.\n\n9. I’d like to delegate this task to...\n\n10. I suggest we meet online next week.\n\n## Wrap-Up\n\nThink about a time you had to ask someone for help at work. What did you say? What polite phrases did you use?",
